(window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS = window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS || {})[6] = {"grade":6,"packs":{"grade6-english":{"g6eng-nouns":[{"id":"g6eng-precision-plurals-15","q":"Two women each brought their son's wife. Which plural correctly names the two relatives?","opts":["daughter-in-law's","daughters-in-law","daughters-in-laws","daughter-in-laws"],"a":"daughters-in-law","hint":"The main noun daughter carries the plural ending.","exp":"The plural is daughters-in-law. The words in-law do not change."},{"id":"g6eng-precision-plurals-4","q":"Correct the noun in brackets: \"Both of the walker's (foot) were covered in mud.\"","opts":["footes","foots","feets","feet"],"a":"feet","hint":"Both refers to two; change the internal vowel.","exp":"Foot becomes feet in the plural. Feet does not take an extra s."},{"id":"g6eng-syl-054","q":"Why does <i>I visited Mauritius last year</i> need no article before \"Mauritius\"?","opts":["It begins with a capital letter","It comes after a verb","It is a long word","It is the name of one place"],"a":"It is the name of one place","hint":"Compare it with \"I visited the island\".","exp":"A proper noun <b>names one particular place</b>, so no article is needed. \"The island\" is a common noun and does need one."},{"id":"g6eng-syl-053","q":"Which sentence is written correctly?","opts":["He went home after work.","He went a home after work.","He went the home after work.","He went to home after work."],"a":"He went home after work.","hint":"\"Home\" behaves differently from most place words.","exp":"\"Go <b>home</b>\" takes neither an article nor \"to\". Words like home, school and bed follow their own rule."},{"id":"g6eng-hd-008","q":"<i>\"The girls bags were left in the teachers room.\"</i> The writer means several girls but only one teacher. Which punctuation says that?","opts":["The girl's bags were left in the teachers' room.","The girls' bags were left in the teacher's room.","The girls's bags were left in the teachers' room.","The girls bag's were left in the teacher's room."],"a":"The girls' bags were left in the teacher's room.","hint":"Write the owner first, then decide where the apostrophe lands.","exp":"Several girls own the bags, so the plural \"girls\" takes the apostrophe <b>after</b> the s. One teacher owns the room, so \"teacher\" takes <b>-'s</b>. The bags themselves own nothing, which is why an apostrophe on \"bags\" is always wrong."}],"g6eng-verbs":[{"id":"g6eng-verb-008","q":"Which is the correct PRESENT PERFECT form?","opts":["She has went shopping.","I have went to the market.","They have go home.","He has gone to school."],"a":"He has gone to school.","hint":"Present perfect = has/have + PAST PARTICIPLE. The past participle of \"go\" is \"gone\".","exp":"\"<b>He has gone to school.</b>\" - \"gone\" is the past participle of \"go\" (go → went → gone). Common error: \"has went\" - \"went\" is past simple, not a past participle. Use \"gone\" with has/have."},{"id":"g6eng-futpun-future-19","q":"Which sentence is grammatically correct?","opts":["I will called you when I arrive.","I will call you as soon as I will arrive.","I will call you as soon as I arrive.","I called you as soon as I will arrive."],"a":"I will call you as soon as I arrive.","hint":"After “as soon as”, use present tense for future time.","exp":"<b>I will call … I arrive</b> uses the correct future time-clause pattern."},{"id":"g6eng-tense-past-18","q":"Choose the sentence with consistent past tenses.","opts":["The guide had open the gate and leads us inside.","The guide opening the gate and lead us inside.","The guide opens the gate and led us inside.","The guide opened the gate and led us inside."],"a":"The guide opened the gate and led us inside.","hint":"Both completed actions belong to the same past sequence.","exp":"<b>Opened</b> and <b>led</b> are consistent simple-past forms."},{"id":"g6eng-syl-041","q":"Why can a phrasal verb be hard to understand from its separate words?","opts":["The pair together has a new meaning","The pair is never used in writing","The small word is always wrong","The verb is always irregular"],"a":"The pair together has a new meaning","hint":"Compare \"give\" and \"up\" with what \"give up\" actually means.","exp":"The two words together take on a <b>new meaning of their own</b>: \"give up\" has nothing to do with giving, and \"run out of\" has nothing to do with running."},{"id":"g6eng-syl-042","q":"A pupil reads <i>The bus broke down on the way to school</i> and thinks something was smashed. What has gone wrong?","opts":["\"Broke down\" means it stopped working","\"Broke down\" means it was late","\"Broke down\" means it crashed","\"Broke down\" means it was full"],"a":"\"Broke down\" means it stopped working","hint":"Take the two words together, not separately.","exp":"<b>Break down</b> means to stop working. Reading the two words separately is exactly the mistake a phrasal verb invites."}],"g6eng-clauses":[{"id":"g6eng-cls-016","q":"Which conjunction introduces a TIME clause?","opts":["although","when","because","so that"],"a":"when","hint":"Time clauses say WHEN something happens in relation to another event.","exp":"\"<b>When</b>\" introduces a time clause: \"When the bell rang, students left the classroom.\" Time conjunctions from MIE Grade 6: <b>when, while, as, before, after, until, as soon as, since, whenever</b>. A time clause tells us the time relationship between two actions. Compare: because (cause), although (concession), so that (purpose)."},{"id":"g6eng-futpun-punct-06","q":"Choose the sentence with the semicolon used correctly.","opts":["The rain; stopped the match continued.","The rain stopped; and the match continued.","The rain stopped; the match continued.","The rain stopped the; match continued."],"a":"The rain stopped; the match continued.","hint":"A semicolon can link two closely related complete clauses.","exp":"Both sides of the semicolon are complete, closely related sentences."},{"id":"g6eng-cls-019","q":"Identify the type of clause in brackets: \"The novel [that was written by Dickens] is still popular today.\"","opts":["A defining relative clause","A main clause","A time clause","A non-defining relative clause"],"a":"A defining relative clause","hint":"Does the clause identify WHICH novel? Are there commas? \"That\" is used - what does that tell you?","exp":"\"[that was written by Dickens]\" is a <b>defining (restrictive) relative clause</b>: (1) it specifies <b>which</b> novel (the one written by Dickens, not any other); (2) there are <b>no commas</b>; (3) it uses \"<b>that</b>\" - which is only used in defining clauses. Without this clause, \"The novel is still popular\" loses its specific meaning. Removing a defining clause changes the meaning; removing a non-defining clause only removes extra information."},{"id":"g6eng-syl-019","q":"A pupil writes: <i>The parcel was too light to carry.</i> Why does the sentence not make sense?","opts":["\"Too\" cannot be used with adjectives","\"Light\" is not an adjective at all","\"Too\" suggests a problem, and light is easy","A parcel cannot be described as light"],"a":"\"Too\" suggests a problem, and light is easy","hint":"Think about what \"too\" always signals about the quality it modifies.","exp":"\"Too\" means more than is wanted, so it must go with a quality that <b>causes a problem</b> &mdash; too heavy to carry. Being light makes carrying easier, so \"too light to carry\" contradicts itself."},{"id":"g6eng-syl-020","q":"Which pair of sentences shows the clearest difference in meaning?","opts":["He is so tall. / He is very tall.","He is quite tall. / He is very tall.","He is really tall. / He is so tall.","He is very tall. / He is really tall."],"a":"He is quite tall. / He is very tall.","hint":"One of these adverbs of degree is weaker than the rest.","exp":"<b>Quite</b> is weaker than very, really and so, which are close in strength. Adverbs of degree are not all equally strong."}]},"grade6-french":{"g6fr-imparfait":[{"id":"g6fr-imp-023","q":"Conjugue PRENDRE à l'imparfait pour \"nous\" :","opts":["nous prendions","nous prenons","nous preniions","nous prenions"],"a":"nous prenions","hint":"Prendre → nous prenons → radical pren- → nous prenions.","exp":"Prendre → présent \"nous\" : nous <b>prenons</b> → radical = <b>pren-</b> → imparfait : je prenais, tu prenais, il prenait, <b>nous prenions</b>, vous preniez, ils prenaient."},{"id":"g6fr-imp-003","q":"Conjuguez \"parler\" à l'imparfait - troisième personne du pluriel (ils) :","opts":["ils parlaient","ils parlent","ils parleront","ils parlait"],"a":"ils parlaient","hint":"Radical : parl- + terminaison de la 3e personne du pluriel.","exp":"Parler → nous parlons → radical <b>parl-</b> + terminaison <b>-aient</b> → <b>ils parlaient</b>. Les terminaisons : je -ais, tu -ais, il -ait, nous -ions, vous -iez, ils <b>-aient</b>."},{"id":"g6fr-imp-026","q":"Conjugue VOULOIR à l'imparfait pour \"tu\" :","opts":["tu veux","tu voulus","tu voulais","tu vouldrais"],"a":"tu voulais","hint":"Le radical de l'imparfait se tire de la forme « nous » du présent de « vouloir ». Ajoute ensuite la terminaison de « tu ».","exp":"Vouloir → présent \"nous\" : nous <b>voulons</b> → radical = <b>voul-</b> → imparfait : je voulais, <b>tu voulais</b>, il voulait, nous voulions, vous vouliez, ils voulaient."},{"id":"g6fr-imp-030","q":"Dans la phrase \"Il était une fois une petite fille qui habitait dans la forêt\", quel est le rôle de l'imparfait ?","opts":["Décrire le cadre et la situation initiale du conte","Décrire des actions rapides et ponctuelles","Exprimer des ordres","Indiquer des actions futures"],"a":"Décrire le cadre et la situation initiale du conte","hint":"Dans les contes, l'imparfait dessine le décor : personnages, lieu, habitudes.","exp":"Dans les contes, l'<b>imparfait descriptif</b> sert à poser le <b>cadre</b> : qui sont les personnages, où ils vivent, leurs habitudes. \"Il <b>était</b> une fois\", \"elle <b>habitait</b>\", \"il <b>aimait</b>\"… Ces descriptions forment l'arrière-plan du récit. Les événements ponctuels, eux, sont au passé composé ou passé simple."},{"id":"g6fr-imp-019","q":"Quel est le radical de l'imparfait pour le verbe \"savoir\" ?","opts":["sach-","sav-","sau-","sai-"],"a":"sav-","hint":"Savoir → nous savons → enlève -ons → ?","exp":"Savoir → forme \"nous\" au présent : nous <b>savons</b> → enlève -ons → radical : <b>sav-</b> → je savais, tu savais, il savait, nous savions, vous saviez, ils savaient. Attention : ne pas confondre avec le subjonctif (que je <b>sache</b>) qui a un radical différent. L'imparfait se forme toujours sur le radical \"nous\" du présent."}],"g6fr-futur":[{"id":"g6fr-fut-012","q":"Conjuguez \"pouvoir\" au futur simple pour \"ils\" :","opts":["ils pourront","ils peuvent","ils pourraient","ils pouvront"],"a":"ils pourront","hint":"Pouvoir → radical irrégulier pourr- + terminaison -ont.","exp":"Pouvoir → radical irrégulier <b>pourr-</b> → + terminaison <b>-ont</b> → <b>ils pourront</b>. Conjugaison : je pourrai, tu pourras, il pourra, nous pourrons, vous pourrez, <b>ils pourront</b>. Autres radicaux irréguliers importants : savoir → <b>saur-</b>, voir → <b>verr-</b>, courir → <b>courr-</b>."},{"id":"g6fr-fut-003","q":"Quel est le radical irrégulier du verbe \"être\" au futur simple ?","opts":["ser-","ét-","êtr-","est-"],"a":"ser-","hint":"Je ___ → je serai.","exp":"<b>Être → ser-</b> au futur. Conjugaison : je <b>serai</b>, tu seras, il sera, nous serons, vous serez, ils seront. Autres radicaux irréguliers : avoir → <b>aur-</b>, aller → <b>ir-</b>, faire → <b>fer-</b>, venir → <b>viendr-</b>."},{"id":"g6fr-fut-027","q":"Conjugue COURIR au futur simple pour \"tu\" :","opts":["tu courrais","tu courras","tu courira","tu couriras"],"a":"tu courras","hint":"Courir → radical irrégulier au futur = courr- (double r).","exp":"Courir → radical irrégulier au futur : <b>courr-</b> (avec double r) → tu <b>courras</b>. Conjugaison : je courrai, <b>tu courras</b>, il courra, nous courrons, vous courrez, ils courront. Attention au double r !"},{"id":"g6fr-fut-029","q":"Conjugue MOURIR au futur simple pour \"nous\" :","opts":["nous mourrons","nous mourrions","nous mourirons","nous mourons"],"a":"nous mourrons","hint":"Mourir → radical irrégulier au futur = mourr- (double r).","exp":"Mourir → radical irrégulier au futur : <b>mourr-</b> (double r) → nous <b>mourrons</b>. Conjugaison : je mourrai, tu mourras, il mourra, <b>nous mourrons</b>, vous mourrez, ils mourront."},{"id":"g6fr-fut-019","q":"Conjuguez \"recevoir\" au futur simple pour \"nous\" :","opts":["nous recevoirons","nous recerons","nous recevrions","nous recevrons"],"a":"nous recevrons","hint":"Recevoir → radical irrégulier recevr- + terminaison -ons.","exp":"Recevoir → radical irrégulier <b>recevr-</b> → + terminaison <b>-ons</b> → <b>nous recevrons</b>. Conjugaison : je recevrai, tu recevras, il recevra, <b>nous recevrons</b>, vous recevrez, ils recevront. De même : apercevoir → nous apercevrons, décevoir → nous décevrons. Groupe des verbes en -cevoir : radical = -cevr-."}],"g6fr-subordonnees":[{"id":"g6fr-sub-024","q":"Transforme au discours indirect : \"Il a dit : 'Je viendrai demain.'\" →","opts":["Il a dit qu'il vient demain.","Il a dit qu'il viendra le lendemain.","Il a dit qu'il viendrait le lendemain.","Il a dit qu'il viendrait demain."],"a":"Il a dit qu'il viendrait le lendemain.","hint":"Verbe introducteur au passé (\"a dit\") → le futur devient conditionnel, et \"demain\" devient \"le lendemain\".","exp":"\"Il <b>a dit</b> (passé) qu'il <b>viendrait</b> le lendemain.\" - Quand le verbe introducteur est au <b>passé</b>, le futur → <b>conditionnel</b>. \"Demain\" → \"<b>le lendemain</b>\" (changement d'expression temporelle)."},{"id":"g6fr-plus-1372","q":"Quel lien logique « lorsque » exprime-t-il dans : « lorsque la cloche sonne, les élèves rentrent en classe. » ?","opts":["le but","une opposition","le moment","la cause"],"a":"le moment","hint":"Cherche si la proposition indique une cause, une opposition, un moment, une condition ou un but.","exp":"Dans cette phrase, « lorsque » exprime le moment."},{"id":"g6fr-plus-1405","q":"Quelle est la proposition principale dans : « si le temps est beau, nous irons à la plage. » ?","opts":["si le temps est beau","nous irons à la plage","Nous sommes restés à la maison","Le match a été annulé"],"a":"nous irons à la plage","hint":"Cherche la proposition qui ne commence pas par le connecteur de subordination.","exp":"La proposition principale est « nous irons à la plage ». L’autre proposition apporte une précision."},{"id":"g6fr-syl-025","q":"Pourquoi peut-on dire qu&rsquo;une phrase a toujours un type <b>et</b> une forme ?","opts":["Le type dépend de la longueur","C&rsquo;est une règle sans raison","La forme remplace le type","Ce sont deux questions différentes"],"a":"Ce sont deux questions différentes","hint":"L&rsquo;une demande « que fait-elle ? », l&rsquo;autre « nie-t-elle ? ».","exp":"Le type répond à « que fait la phrase ? » et la forme à « affirme-t-elle ou nie-t-elle ? ». <b>Ce sont deux questions différentes</b>, donc les deux réponses existent toujours."},{"id":"g6fr-syl-036","q":"Pourquoi « Mais où est donc Ornicar ? » aide-t-il à retenir les conjonctions de coordination ?","opts":["C&rsquo;est une phrase impérative","Elle contient sept verbes","C&rsquo;est le titre d&rsquo;un livre connu","Chaque son de la phrase en rappelle une"],"a":"Chaque son de la phrase en rappelle une","hint":"Prononce la phrase lentement et écoute les sept mots.","exp":"Chaque son rappelle une conjonction : <b>mais, ou, et, donc, or, ni, car</b>. C&rsquo;est un moyen mnémotechnique, pas une règle."}]},"grade6-history":{"g6-slaves-immigrants":[{"id":"g6h-pp21-005","q":"Which one of the following islands was known as <b>Ile Bourbon</b> in the past?","opts":["Rodrigues","Madagascar","Réunion","Mauritius"],"a":"Réunion","hint":"In the past, nearby islands had different French names. Think about which island near Mauritius was called \"Ile Bourbon.\"","exp":"Réunion was known as Ile Bourbon during the French colonial period. Mauritius was known as Ile de France, and Madagascar was known as Ile Dauphine."},{"id":"g6h-why-016","q":"Give one reason why some enslaved people <b>ran away</b> from the estates.","opts":["Because the estates were closing down","Because they were given too much food","To take up paid work in the towns","To escape harsh treatment and long hours"],"a":"To escape harsh treatment and long hours","hint":"Think about the conditions on a sugar estate under slavery.","exp":"Enslaved people worked from dawn to dusk, were poorly fed and were punished severely. Those who escaped were called <b>maroons</b>, and many hid in the mountains, including Le Morne."},{"id":"g6hg-si-029","q":"What was daily life like for the small number of people living here under Dutch rule?","opts":["Quiet, with no work to do","Easy, with plenty of everything","Hard, with few people and little food","Rich, with large towns and markets"],"a":"Hard, with few people and little food","hint":"Their settlement stayed tiny for seventy years and then was abandoned.","exp":"The Dutch settlement was small and isolated, food was short and cyclones frequent, so life was <b>hard</b>. That is why it never grew into a colony."},{"id":"g6hg-si-041","q":"Without the work of the slaves, the early sugar colony could not have existed. Why not?","opts":["No ships would call at the island","Sugar had not yet been invented","There was nobody else to do the heavy work","The soil would not grow cane"],"a":"There was nobody else to do the heavy work","hint":"Count how many settlers there were, and how much land had to be cleared by hand.","exp":"The settlers were few and the work of clearing, planting, cutting and carrying was enormous, so <b>the whole colony rested on the labour of the slaves</b>. That is why their contribution is remembered."},{"id":"g6hg-si-030","q":"The Dutch stayed about seventy years and left almost no towns behind, yet they changed the island for ever. How?","opts":["They divided the island into nine districts","They opened the first schools and hospitals here","They built the capital city and its main harbour","Cane, deer and rats came; ebony and dodo went"],"a":"Cane, deer and rats came; ebony and dodo went","hint":"Think about what arrived with them and what disappeared while they were here.","exp":"Their lasting mark was on the <b>living things</b>: sugar cane, deer and rats arrived, while the ebony forests were cut and the dodo was lost. Port Louis and the districts came later, under the French."}],"g6-independence":[{"id":"g6hg-ind-013","q":"What does the YELLOW stripe on the Mauritian flag represent?","opts":["The Indian Ocean","The light of independence","The struggle for independence","The vegetation of the island"],"a":"The light of independence","hint":"Yellow is often associated with light, hope and a bright future.","exp":"The <b>yellow stripe</b> represents the <b>light of independence</b> - the new hope and bright future that independence brought to Mauritius. It also symbolises the <b>sun</b> shining on a prosperous nation. Together, the four colours tell the story of independence (red), the ocean environment (blue), hope (yellow) and the land's fertility (green)."},{"id":"g6h-pp19-016","q":"Which feature on the Coat of Arms of Mauritius shows that Mauritius is a <b>tropical island</b>?","opts":["The sugar cane","The dodo bird","The sambar deer","The ship"],"a":"The sugar cane","hint":"This crop is grown in warm, tropical climates and dominates Mauritius's landscape.","exp":"The sugar cane plants on the Coat of Arms show that Mauritius is a tropical island, as sugar cane grows in warm, tropical climates. It is also the main crop and industry of Mauritius."},{"id":"g6h-why-020","q":"Why is it important for citizens to know their country's <b>national symbols</b>?","opts":["They are needed to travel to other countries","They decide which language people speak","They are printed on every school textbook","They stand for the country's history and identity"],"a":"They stand for the country's history and identity","hint":"What does a flag or a coat of arms actually tell you about a nation?","exp":"The flag, coat of arms, anthem and national flower carry the <b>story and values of the nation</b> - its struggle, its ocean, its wildlife. Knowing them is part of belonging to the country."},{"id":"g6hg-hd-015","q":"On a map of Mauritius the key shows a small aeroplane, an anchor and a star. The star has been placed on Port Louis.<br>What is the star most likely marking?","opts":["A deep harbour used by large ships","A place where aeroplanes can land","The highest mountain shown on the map","The capital city of the country"],"a":"The capital city of the country","hint":"Two of the other symbols already have their own meanings in that key.","exp":"The aeroplane marks the airport and the anchor the harbour, so the star must mean something else. A star on a settlement is the usual sign for a <b>capital</b>."},{"id":"g6hg-ind-019","q":"A pupil says the blue stripe on the Mauritian flag stands for the sky. Which correction is right?","opts":["Blue stands for the night sky","Blue stands for the police force","Blue stands for our fresh water","Blue stands for the Indian Ocean"],"a":"Blue stands for the Indian Ocean","hint":"Each colour has a specific symbolic meaning that reflects Mauritius's history and identity.","exp":"The four stripes form a narrative: <b>Red</b> = the blood and struggle for independence (colonial hardship, activism); <b>Blue</b> = the Indian Ocean that defines the island's geography and identity; <b>Yellow</b> = the light of freedom, a bright future; <b>Green</b> = the fertile agricultural land, particularly the sugar cane that shaped the island's economy and culture. Together they are a symbolic autobiography of the nation."}],"g6-cultural-heritage":[{"id":"g6h-pp19-017","q":"Which of the following museums in Mauritius was created to commemorate the <b>Dutch settlement</b> and the first European landing?","opts":["Aapravasi Ghat Museum","National History Museum","Blue Penny Museum","Fort Frederik Hendrik Museum"],"a":"Fort Frederik Hendrik Museum","hint":"The name \"Frederik Hendrik\" is Dutch, and this museum is at Vieux Grand Port.","exp":"The Fort Frederik Hendrik Museum at Vieux Grand Port commemorates the Dutch settlement of Mauritius (1638–1710) and the first European landing on the island."},{"id":"g6h-pp24-016","q":"The Slave Route Monument is found at the foot of which mountain in Mauritius?","opts":["Corps de Garde","Le Morne Brabant","Trois Mamelles","Pieter Both"],"a":"Le Morne Brabant","hint":"This mountain is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and a symbol of slave resistance.","exp":"The Slave Route Monument is at the foot of Le Morne Brabant mountain. Le Morne was a refuge for runaway slaves (maroons) and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."},{"id":"g6h-pp22-017","q":"Which museum, situated in Port Louis, contains natural history specimens and was built during the colonial period?","opts":["Martello Tower Museum","Blue Penny Museum","Folk Museum","Natural History Museum"],"a":"Natural History Museum","hint":"This museum in Port Louis is famous for its specimens of wildlife, including an extinct bird unique to Mauritius.","exp":"The Natural History Museum in Port Louis houses important natural history specimens, including the skeleton and models of the extinct dodo bird."},{"id":"g6h-why-018","q":"Why are <b>museums</b> important to a country?","opts":["They keep objects from the past for everyone","They are the cheapest buildings to run","They are only useful to visiting tourists","They replace the need for history lessons"],"a":"They keep objects from the past for everyone","hint":"What would be lost if a country had no museum at all?","exp":"A museum <b>preserves objects, documents and specimens</b> that cannot be replaced, and puts them where everyone - pupils, researchers and visitors - can learn from them."},{"id":"g6hg-dep-121","q":"Why is it important to protect historical sites?","opts":["They tell us about our past","They provide farmland","They increase the rainfall","They stop cyclones forming"],"a":"They tell us about our past","hint":"Think about what is lost if they disappear.","exp":"Historical sites <b>tell us about our past</b>. Once destroyed they cannot be replaced, and the story they carry is lost."}]},"grade6-maths":{"g6-numeration":[{"id":"g6m-pp24-028","q":"(7 × 10) + (6 × 1) + (8 × 100) + (1 × 1 000) =","opts":["8 176","8 716","1 876","1 867"],"a":"1 876","hint":"Calculate each term: 70 + 6 + 800 + 1 000.","exp":"70 + 6 + 800 + 1 000 = 1 876."},{"id":"g6m-num-018","q":"A number has the digit 8 in the hundred-thousands place, 0 in the ten-thousands, 4 in the thousands, 7 in the hundreds, 0 in the tens and 3 in the ones. What is the number?","opts":["840,703","804,073","804,703","840,073"],"a":"804,703","hint":"Build the number: HTh=8, TTh=0, Th=4, H=7, T=0, O=3.","exp":"HTh=8 → 800,000; TTh=0 → 0; Th=4 → 4,000; H=7 → 700; T=0 → 0; O=3 → 3. Answer: <b>804,703</b>."},{"id":"g6m-pp21-016","q":"(5 × 100) + (4 × 1 000) + (7 × 1) + (3 × 10) =","opts":["4537","5473","5437","4573"],"a":"4537","hint":"Work out each bracket: 500 + 4000 + 7 + 30.","exp":"500 + 4 000 + 7 + 30 = 4 537."},{"id":"g6m-pp19-020","q":"2² × 2<sup>□</sup> = 2³. The <b>missing power</b> is","opts":["1","0","3","2"],"a":"1","hint":"4 × 2□ = 8. So 2□ = 2. What power of 2 equals 2?","exp":"2¹ = 2. So the missing power is 1."},{"id":"g6m-num-008","q":"What is the number 700,000 + 40,000 + 3,000 + 200 + 80 + 5?","opts":["743,825","740,285","743,205","743,285"],"a":"743,285","hint":"Line the parts up by place value - hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, units - then read the digits off in order.","exp":"700,000 + 40,000 + 3,000 + 200 + 80 + 5 = <b>743,285</b>."}],"g6-four-ops":[{"id":"g6m-ops-006","q":"Using BODMAS, calculate: (8 + 4) ÷ 3 × 2","opts":["8","6","24","4"],"a":"8","hint":"Brackets first: (8+4)=12. Then left to right: 12÷3=4, then 4×2=8.","exp":"Brackets first: (8 + 4) = 12. Then left to right: 12 ÷ 3 = 4, then 4 × 2 = <b>8</b>."},{"id":"g6m-pp23-027","q":"A shopkeeper buys 150 kg of rice and puts it into small packets. Each small packet weighs 1 250 g. How many such small packets does the shopkeeper get?","opts":["125","110","100","120"],"a":"120","hint":"Convert 150 kg to grams first, then divide.","exp":"150 kg = 150 000 g. 150 000 ÷ 1 250 = 120 packets."},{"id":"g6m-ops-018","q":"A factory worker earns Rs 450 per day. He works 5 days a week for 52 weeks. However, he takes 12 days of unpaid leave. What are his TOTAL EARNINGS for the year?","opts":["Rs 116,550","Rs 105,600","Rs 117,000","Rs 111,600"],"a":"Rs 111,600","hint":"Total working days = (52 × 5) − 12. Earnings = working days × Rs 450.","exp":"Total scheduled days = 52 × 5 = 260. Days worked = 260 − 12 = 248. Earnings = 248 × 450 = <b>Rs 111,600</b>."},{"id":"g6m-pp21-037","q":"Box A contains <b>3 times</b> as many balls as Box B. Box C contains <b>half</b> as many as Box B. There are 36 <b>more</b> balls in Box A than in Box B. Find the number of balls in Box B.","opts":["36","24","12","18"],"a":"18","hint":"A = 3B. A − B = 36. Solve: 3B − B = 36.","exp":"3B − B = 2B = 36. B = 18."},{"id":"g6m-pp23-035","q":"4 pens and 6 pencils cost Rs 172. 2 pens and 2 pencils cost Rs 74. Find the cost of <b>1 pencil</b>.","opts":["Rs 12","Rs 6","Rs 25","Rs 31"],"a":"Rs 12","hint":"From the second equation, find the cost of 1 pen + 1 pencil. Then use the first equation.","exp":"2p+2c=74 → p+c=37 → p=37−c. Into 4p+6c=172: 4(37−c)+6c=172 → 148+2c=172 → 2c=24 → c=Rs 12."}],"g6-fractions":[{"id":"g6m-frac-008","q":"Which fraction is GREATER: 3/4 or 5/7?","opts":["They are equal","5/7","Cannot be determined","3/4"],"a":"3/4","hint":"Convert both to the same denominator (LCM of 4 and 7 = 28). 3/4=21/28, 5/7=20/28.","exp":"LCM(4,7)=28. 3/4 = 21/28. 5/7 = 20/28. Since 21/28 > 20/28, <b>3/4 is greater</b>."},{"id":"g6m-pp21-023","q":"Work out: 24 ÷ <sup>2</sup>/<sub>3</sub>","opts":["16","48","40","36"],"a":"36","hint":"Dividing by a fraction is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal.","exp":"24 ÷ 2/3 = 24 × 3/2 = 72/2 = 36."},{"id":"g6m-pp24-032","q":"Work out &nbsp;<sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>45</sub> ÷ <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>15</sub>,&nbsp; giving your answer in its <b>simplest form</b>.","opts":["<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>","<sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>","<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub>","<sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>675</sub>"],"a":"<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub>","hint":"To divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal: 4/45 × 15/8.","exp":"4/45 × 15/8 = 60/360 = 1/6."},{"id":"g6m-pp21-036","q":"Wali has 3/5 of his biscuits left. On Tuesday, he eats <sup>1</sup>/<sub>4</sub> of the <b>remaining</b> biscuits. What fraction of biscuits is left in the box?","opts":["1/4","3/20","11/20","9/20"],"a":"9/20","hint":"Eat 1/4 of 3/5. Then subtract from 3/5.","exp":"Eaten Tuesday = 1/4 × 3/5 = 3/20. Left = 3/5 − 3/20 = 12/20 − 3/20 = 9/20."},{"id":"g6m-pp19-032","q":"On a farm, 5/9 of the animals are chickens and 1/4 of the <b>remaining</b> animals are goats; the rest are ducks. If there are 27 ducks, find the <b>total</b> number of animals on the farm.","opts":["54","72","81","108"],"a":"81","hint":"Remaining after chickens = 4/9. Goats = 1/4 × 4/9 = 1/9. Ducks = 4/9 − 1/9 = 3/9 = 1/3.","exp":"Ducks = 1/3 of total. Total = 27 × 3 = 81."}]},"grade6-science":{"g6-air":[{"id":"g6sc-dep-120","q":"Which gas is present in air but NOT in dry air?","opts":["Water vapour","Nitrogen","Oxygen","Carbon dioxide"],"a":"Water vapour","hint":"The word \"dry\" is the clue.","exp":"Dry air has had its <b>water vapour</b> removed. Ordinary air always contains some water vapour."},{"id":"g6sc-pp22-015","q":"Ahmad places a live cockroach in a closed jar. After some time, what happens to the amounts of oxygen and carbon dioxide in the jar?","opts":["Oxygen increases; carbon dioxide decreases","Oxygen decreases; carbon dioxide decreases","Oxygen increases; carbon dioxide increases","Oxygen decreases; carbon dioxide increases"],"a":"Oxygen decreases; carbon dioxide increases","hint":"The cockroach is alive and breathing inside the sealed jar.","exp":"The cockroach breathes - it uses up oxygen and releases carbon dioxide through respiration. In a closed jar, oxygen decreases and carbon dioxide increases over time."},{"id":"g6sc-dep-128","q":"Which gas do all living things take in during respiration?","opts":["Nitrogen","Oxygen","Water vapour","Carbon dioxide"],"a":"Oxygen","hint":"It is the opposite of what plants take in for photosynthesis.","exp":"In respiration all living things take in <b>oxygen</b> and release carbon dioxide, day and night."},{"id":"g6sc-hd-001","q":"An inflated balloon is left in a hot car. It bursts.<br>What happened inside it?","opts":["The rubber shrank in the heat","The air particles spread out and pushed harder","More air leaked into the balloon from the outside","The air inside became heavier"],"a":"The air particles spread out and pushed harder","hint":"Heating a gas changes how its particles move.","exp":"Heat makes the particles move faster and spread, so they <b>strike the walls harder and more often</b>. Pressure rises until the rubber gives way. No extra air entered — the same air simply pushes harder."},{"id":"g6sc-hd-086","q":"At night the land cools faster than the sea, and the breeze reverses to blow from land to sea.<br>What does that tell you about the daytime breeze?","opts":["It is caused by the tide rather than by heat","It blows the same way both day and night","It only happens when there are clouds about","It blew inland because the land was warmer then"],"a":"It blew inland because the land was warmer then","hint":"The breeze always blows toward the warmer side. Which side is warmer by day?","exp":"Air rises over the <b>warmer</b> surface and cooler air flows in beneath. By day the land is warmer, so the breeze comes off the sea; at night that reverses. The reversal proves heat drives it."}],"g6-materials":[{"id":"g6sc-dep-038","q":"Which two things are needed for iron to rust?","opts":["Air and light","Light and heat","Water and salt","Air and water"],"a":"Air and water","hint":"A nail in dry air stays clean; so does one in boiled, sealed water.","exp":"Rusting needs both <b>air (oxygen) and water</b>. Remove either one and the iron does not rust."},{"id":"g6sc-dep-029","q":"Why is the roof of a house often made of corrugated iron sheets?","opts":["They are transparent","They conduct electricity","They are strong and keep rain out","They absorb rainwater"],"a":"They are strong and keep rain out","hint":"A roof has two main jobs.","exp":"Iron sheets are <b>strong and keep rain out</b>. They are usually painted, because bare iron would rust."},{"id":"g6sc-dep-013","q":"Give one reason why plastic has replaced glass for many bottles.","opts":["It melts in the sun","It rusts in the rain","It does not break easily","It is a natural material"],"a":"It does not break easily","hint":"Think about dropping a bottle on the floor.","exp":"Plastic is light and <b>does not break easily</b>, so it is safer and cheaper to carry than glass."},{"id":"g6sc-hd-093","q":"A town separates its rubbish into paper, glass, metal and food waste.<br>Why is sorting done before recycling rather than after?","opts":["Sorting makes the rubbish weigh much less","Mixed waste is far harder to process cleanly","Recycling machines cannot run on clean waste","Separated bins take up less room in a lorry"],"a":"Mixed waste is far harder to process cleanly","hint":"Think about glass fragments inside recycled paper.","exp":"Each material needs a <b>different process</b>, and contamination ruins a batch — a little glass in the paper stream spoils it. Sorting at the source is far cheaper than separating later."},{"id":"g6sc-hd-091","q":"A banana skin buried in a garden disappears within weeks; a plastic bag buried beside it is still there years later.<br>What is the difference?","opts":["The plastic bag was buried much deeper down","Plastic bags are far heavier than banana skins","Living things can break down one but not the other","Banana skins dissolve in the water in the soil"],"a":"Living things can break down one but not the other","hint":"Something in the soil eats one of them.","exp":"Decomposers can digest the banana skin — it is <b>biodegradable</b>. Plastic is not, so it stays in the ground. That is why plastic waste is a problem of time, not of size."}],"g6-animals":[{"id":"g6sc-dep-065","q":"Which layer covers and protects the outside of a tooth?","opts":["Nerve","Enamel","Gum","Root"],"a":"Enamel","hint":"It is the hardest part of the body.","exp":"<b>Enamel</b> is the hard white layer covering the crown of the tooth. It protects the softer parts inside."},{"id":"g6sc-dep-094","q":"Give one reason why a growing child needs more protein than an old person.","opts":["The child drinks less water","The child is still building its body","The child cannot digest fat","The child needs less energy"],"a":"The child is still building its body","hint":"What is protein used for?","exp":"Protein builds new body tissue, and <b>a child is still building its body</b>, so it needs a larger share."},{"id":"g6sc-dep-108","q":"Trees are cut down in a forest. What happens to the animals living there?","opts":["They change into another group","They stop needing water","They lose their shelter and food","They grow larger and stronger"],"a":"They lose their shelter and food","hint":"Their habitat has been destroyed.","exp":"They <b>lose their shelter and food</b>, so they must move away or they die. This is why deforestation threatens wildlife."},{"id":"g6sc-hd-097","q":"A fish taken from the lagoon and put into fresh water soon dies, although both are water.<br>Why?","opts":["Fresh water contains no oxygen for fish","Fresh water is always far too cold","The fish cannot swim in still fresh water","Its body is suited to salty water only"],"a":"Its body is suited to salty water only","hint":"Water is not just water — what is dissolved in it matters.","exp":"A sea fish's body is adapted to <b>salty surroundings</b> and cannot control its water balance in fresh water. A habitat is defined by conditions, not by the substance alone."},{"id":"g6sc-hd-094","q":"A skull is found with long sharp pointed teeth at the front and no flat grinding teeth at all.<br>What did the animal most likely eat?","opts":["Leaves, because sharp teeth strip them from twigs","Meat, because the teeth are for gripping and tearing","Fruit, because soft food needs no flat teeth","Grass, because pointed teeth cut stems easily"],"a":"Meat, because the teeth are for gripping and tearing","hint":"Match the tooth shape to the job it can do.","exp":"Pointed teeth grip and tear, which suits <b>meat</b>. Plant food must be ground down, and this skull has no grinding teeth — the absence is as informative as what is there."}]}}};
